<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Don’t Pass@<math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mohsen Hariri
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Amirhossein Samandar
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Michael Hinczewski
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vipin Chaudhary<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>Department of Computer and Data Sciences, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Department of Physics, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{mohsen.hariri, axs2935, mxh605, vipin}@case.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Pass@<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> is widely used to report performance for LLM reasoning, but it often yields unstable, misleading rankings, especially when the number of trials (samples) is limited and compute is constrained. We present a principled Bayesian evaluation framework that replaces Pass@<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> and average accuracy over <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> trials (avg@<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math>) with posterior estimates of a model’s underlying success probability and credible intervals, yielding stable rankings and a transparent decision rule for differences. Evaluation outcomes are modeled as categorical (not just 0/1) with a Dirichlet prior, giving closed-form expressions for the posterior mean and uncertainty of any weighted rubric and enabling the use of prior evidence when appropriate. Theoretically, under a uniform prior, the Bayesian posterior mean is order-equivalent to average accuracy (Pass@<math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!1</annotation></semantics></math>), explaining its empirical robustness while adding principled uncertainty. Empirically, in simulations with known ground-truth success rates and on AIME’24/’25, HMMT’25, and BrUMO’25, the Bayesian/avg procedure achieves faster convergence and greater rank stability than Pass@<math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> and recent variants, enabling reliable comparisons at far smaller sample counts. The framework clarifies when observed gaps are statistically meaningful (non-overlapping credible intervals) versus noise, and it naturally extends to graded, rubric-based evaluations. Together, these results recommend replacing Pass@<math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> for LLM evaluation and ranking with a posterior-based, compute-efficient protocol that unifies binary and non-binary evaluation while making uncertainty explicit. Source code is available at <a href="https://mohsenhariri.github.io/bayes-kit" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://mohsenhariri.github.io/bayes-kit</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have moved rapidly from research artifacts to everyday infrastructure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a></cite>. Students use them for homework and exam preparation; developers rely on them for code synthesis and refactoring  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">StackOverflow (2025)</a></cite>; analysts and clinicians use them for decision support; and agents built atop LLMs are increasingly embedded in workflows across industry and government. This demand has catalyzed unprecedented investment: specialized chips, datacenters, and startups dedicated to LLM training, serving, and tooling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maslej et al. (2025)</a></cite>. As deployment accelerates, trust, oversight, and comparability become central: <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how we evaluate LLMs</em> directly shapes which models are adopted, what progress is declared, and how resources are allocated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srivastava et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffmann et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation, however, remains the weakest link in the LLM pipeline. Alongside advances in model efficiency and compression<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, training and fine-tuning (PEFT/LoRA, RL-from-human-feedback) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and inference/decoding (sampling strategies, caching, efficient attention) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao, 2023</a>)</cite>, the community still leans on simple, yet flawed, success rates and Pass@<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math>-style metrics to summarize capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>. These practices are convenient but fragile. On small or costly benchmarks (e.g., math reasoning sets with only tens of problems such as AIME) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">MAA, 2025</a>)</cite>, Pass@<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> or single-run accuracy often produce unstable rankings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hochlehnert et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, are sensitive to decoding choices and seed effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hochlehnert et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and provide little guidance on whether observed gaps are meaningful or mere noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dror et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeh, 2000</a>)</cite>. Averaging across multiple runs (“avg@<math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math>”) helps but is compute-hungry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dodge et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, offers no unified way to handle graded/rubric outcomes, and lacks a principled decision rule for significance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dror et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper takes a different approach: we treat evaluation itself as a statistical inference problem. We introduce a <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">posterior-based</em> framework that replaces Pass@<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> and avg@<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math> with estimates of a model’s underlying success probabilities and associated uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Outcomes are modeled as <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">categorical</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hayden and Armitage (2025)</a></cite> rather than purely binary: each item can yield correct, partially correct, formatting-error, refusal, or rubric-defined levels. A Dirichlet prior over these categories yields closed-form posterior means and credible intervals for any <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weighted rubric</em>, allowing the evaluator to report both a point estimate and principled uncertainty with negligible overhead. In the binary special case under a uniform prior, its posterior mean is order-equivalent to average accuracy, explaining the empirical robustness of avg@<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> while making uncertainty explicit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The framework addresses <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">four</em> persistent pain points. ➊ <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Convergence</em>: as shown in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, we ideally want methods that can converge to the true underlying ranking with the smallest number of trials, but different approaches can have significantly different convergence speeds. ➋
<em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Credible intervals</em>: a simple, transparent rule—<span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do not declare a winner when intervals overlap</span>—reduces leaderboard churn and over-interpretation of tiny gaps by introducing a compute-efficient confidence interval (CI). Updates are analytic; one can monitor interval widths online, and allocate additional trials only when needed (no Monte Carlo/bootstrap simulations are required for CI estimation). ➌ <em id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Categorical evaluation</em>: our approach unifies binary and non-binary evaluation. Graded rubrics are natural in this framework, so one can evaluate step-by-step reasoning, partial credit, or judge categories without ad hoc aggregation. ➍ <em id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Prior information</em>: we can incorporate prior evidence when appropriate (e.g., reuse of stable rubric distributions across closely related tasks or versions).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We validate the approach in two settings: In controlled simulations with known ground-truth success rates, the posterior procedure converges to correct rankings with fewer samples than Pass@<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and recent variants, and it flags when ties are statistically unresolved. On real math-reasoning benchmarks (AIME’24/’25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathematical Association of America (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathematical Association of America (2025)</a></cite>, HMMT’25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harvard–MIT Mathematics Tournament (2025)</a></cite>, and BrUMO’25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown University Math Olympiad Organizers (2025)</a></cite>-derived sets), we observe the same pattern: the posterior method achieves greater rank stability at far smaller sample counts than Pass@<math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math>, while clarifying when differences are meaningful versus noise. Practically, this yields a computationally efficient protocol that is easy to implement and audit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our contributions as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A unified Bayesian evaluation framework.</span> We model per-item outcomes as categorical with a Dirichlet prior, yielding closed-form posterior means and credible intervals for <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> weighted rubric, with binary evaluation as a special case. This unifies 0/1 and graded evaluations and supports reuse of prior evidence when justified.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A compute-efficient, interval-aware protocol.</span> We provide a simple recipe: report posterior means with credible intervals; only declare differences when intervals do not overlap; adaptively allocate additional samples until intervals meet pre-specified widths. This protocol naturally supports sequential/online evaluation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical evidence on simulations and math benchmarks.</span> On synthetic data with known ground truth and on AIME’24/’25, HMMT’25, and BrUMO’25 datasets, our method achieves faster convergence and greater rank stability than Pass@<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> and recent variants, enabling reliable comparisons with far fewer samples.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Background: The Pass@<math id="S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> Metric and Its Limitations</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation metrics for LLMs aim to quantify performance on tasks like reasoning or programming, but they often struggle to provide reliable relative rankings across models. Pass@<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, for instance, estimates the probability of at least one correct answer within <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> model attempts (see <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Extended Related Work ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for details). While convenient, this metric exhibits high variance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dalal et al. (2025)</a></cite>, particularly when <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> approaches the total number of trials, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in unstable rankings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021b)</a></cite>. Small fluctuations in correctness can distort comparisons, particularly in benchmarks with few problems or limited computational resources, raising doubts about its suitability for differentiating model capabilities. If a metric cannot consistently distinguish stronger models from weaker ones, its value as a benchmarking tool is undermined <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024a)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Estimating uncertainty in Pass@<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!k</annotation></semantics></math> scores is also challenging, as it lacks closed-form expressions for variance, relying instead on computationally intensive approximations like bootstrapping. A truly effective metric should yield reliable performance rankings with a minimal number of trials, prioritizing both accuracy and efficiency in resource-constrained environments. To address these limitations, we propose a Bayesian evaluation framework that provides more stable estimates of performance, incorporates uncertainty, and facilitates robust relative comparisons across models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ross et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vashurin et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Results Matrix</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a results matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> for an LLM evaluated on a test set comprising <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> questions. Due to the stochastic nature of LLM sampling, responses may vary across independent trials, so we run the LLM <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> times per question. The outcomes are captured in the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times N</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, where element <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the score in the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th trial for the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>th question. This score is an integer ranging from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to a maximum value <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting a rating system with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C+1</annotation></semantics></math> categories. In the binary case (<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>), 0 indicates an incorrect answer and 1 a correct one, though we accommodate more nuanced rubrics generally.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Weighted Performance Metric</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>th question, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1,\ldots,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1,\ldots,M</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an underlying probability <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha k}</annotation></semantics></math> that the LLM’s answer falls in the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th category. We denote <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector with elements <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,\ldots,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,\ldots,C</annotation></semantics></math>. If all <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> were known, we could calculate a desired performance metric <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> as a weighted average over these probabilities:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\bm{w}\cdot\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\sum_{k=0}^{C}w_{k}\pi_{\alpha k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\bm{w}\cdot\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\sum_{k=0}^{C}w_{k}\pi_{\alpha k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector of constant weights. For example, if <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}=k</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the average category label. In the case where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math>, this average corresponds to the mean probability of a correct answer over the entire test set. However, we allow for a general choice of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}</annotation></semantics></math> to accommodate a wide range of possible metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Bayesian Estimator and Uncertainty for the Performance Metric</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In principle, we could estimate <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> by running an arbitrarily large number of trials with the LLM, yielding an accurate estimate of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we are typically constrained to small <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> due to limited computational resources. Our goal is to develop a Bayesian approach to estimate <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> and its associated uncertainty given a finite <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. The first step is to construct <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}(\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}|\bm{R}_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}(\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}|\bm{R}_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>, the posterior probability of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> given the <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>th row of the matrix <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{R}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{R}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. This posterior depends on the data in <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{R}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{R}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and a chosen prior distribution <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}(\bm{\pi}_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}(\bm{\pi}_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> for the unknown underlying probability vector <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. The prior could be uniform (assuming no prior information) or incorporate previously gathered evidence about the LLM’s performance. The Bayesian framework focuses on two quantities: the first is the mean of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> over the joint posterior for all questions, which we denote as <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a Bayesian optimal estimator, minimizing the quadratic loss function <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}(\bar{\pi}^{\text{est}})={\mathbb{E}}_{R,\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}}(\bar{\pi}^{\text{est}}(R)-\bar{\pi})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>est</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>est</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}(\bar{\pi}^{\text{est}})={\mathbb{E}}_{R,\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}}(\bar{\pi}^{\text{est}}(R)-\bar{\pi})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over all possible estimators <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}^{\text{est}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>est</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}^{\text{est}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the expectation value is over all possible <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and realizations of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaynes (2003)</a></cite>. The second quantity is the variance <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>,
which quantifies the uncertainty of the <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> estimate. Both <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> have exact closed-form expressions, derived in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Derivation of Bayesian Estimator and Uncertainty ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, and can be simply calculated for any <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> using Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Bayesian Estimator and Uncertainty for the Performance Metric ‣ 2 Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> LLM performance evaluation using the Bayes@<math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math> framework.</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">function</span> <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EvaluatePerformance</span>(<math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R^{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R^{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">input:</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times N</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of results, with each element <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha i}=0,\ldots,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha i}=0,\ldots,C</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
                  weight vector <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}=(w_{0},\ldots,w_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}=(w_{0},\ldots,w_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> defining performance metric <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">optional input:</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times D</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of results for prior; otherwise <math id="alg1.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">output:</span> performance metric estimate <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and associated uncertainty <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1+C+D+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1+C+D+N</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Tally results in <math id="alg1.l7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\alpha k}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\delta_{k,R_{\alpha i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\alpha k}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\delta_{k,R_{\alpha i}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{0}_{\alpha k}=1+\sum_{i=1}^{D}\delta_{k,R^{0}_{\alpha i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{0}_{\alpha k}=1+\sum_{i=1}^{D}\delta_{k,R^{0}_{\alpha i}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\alpha k}=n^{0}_{\alpha k}+n_{\alpha k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\alpha k}=n^{0}_{\alpha k}+n_{\alpha k}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=w_{0}+\frac{1}{MT}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\sum_{j=0}^{C}\nu_{\alpha j}(w_{j}-w_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=w_{0}+\frac{1}{MT}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\sum_{j=0}^{C}\nu_{\alpha j}(w_{j}-w_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\left[\frac{1}{M^{2}(T+1)}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\left\{\sum_{j=0}^{C}\frac{\nu_{\alpha j}}{T}(w_{j}-w_{0})^{2}-\left(\sum_{j=0}^{C}\frac{\nu_{\alpha j}}{T}(w_{j}-w_{0})\right)^{2}\right\}\right]^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\left[\frac{1}{M^{2}(T+1)}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{M}\left\{\sum_{j=0}^{C}\frac{\nu_{\alpha j}}{T}(w_{j}-w_{0})^{2}-\left(\sum_{j=0}^{C}\frac{\nu_{\alpha j}}{T}(w_{j}-w_{0})\right)^{2}\right\}\right]^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">function</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Using Uncertainty Estimates to Decide Significance of Performance Differences</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, the expressions for <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> are valid for any <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and do not rely on asymptotic arguments like the central limit theorem (CLT). However, there are useful simplifications that occur in specific limiting cases. For example as the size of the test set <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> becomes large, we can derive not just the moments of the posterior distribution for <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, but also its shape, which becomes approximately Gaussian:
<math id="S2.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}(\bar{\pi}|R)\sim{\cal N}(\mu(R),\sigma^{2}(R))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}(\bar{\pi}|R)\sim{\cal N}(\mu(R),\sigma^{2}(R))</annotation></semantics></math>. This allows us to assess whether two methods exhibit a statistically significant performance difference. Consider results matrices <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from two approaches, with corresponding means <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and standard deviations <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The distribution of the performance difference <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\bar{\pi}\equiv\bar{\pi}-\bar{\pi}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\bar{\pi}\equiv\bar{\pi}-\bar{\pi}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a convolution of the individual posteriors, yielding another normal distribution: <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}(\Delta\bar{\pi}|R,R^{\prime})\sim{\cal N}(\tilde{\mu},\tilde{\sigma}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}(\Delta\bar{\pi}|R,R^{\prime})\sim{\cal N}(\tilde{\mu},\tilde{\sigma}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the mean of the difference is <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mu}=\mu-\mu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mu}=\mu-\mu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the standard deviation is <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}=\sqrt{\sigma^{2}+(\sigma^{\prime})^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}=\sqrt{\sigma^{2}+(\sigma^{\prime})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To determine our confidence in the ranking of the two methods, we need to determine the probability that <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}(\Delta\bar{\pi})=\text{sign}(\mu-\mu^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}(\Delta\bar{\pi})=\text{sign}(\mu-\mu^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be done by calculating the absolute <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score, <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=|\mu-\mu^{\prime}|/\sqrt{\sigma^{2}+(\sigma^{\prime})^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=|\mu-\mu^{\prime}|/\sqrt{\sigma^{2}+(\sigma^{\prime})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability that the ranking based on <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is correct (the ranking confidence <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) is given by <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=(1/2)(1+\text{erf}(z/\sqrt{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>erf</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=(1/2)(1+\text{erf}(z/\sqrt{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>. For example <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=1.645" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.645</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=1.645</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span>Equivalence of Bayesian and Average Rankings for Uniform Prior</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the results below, we will denote ranking based on the Bayesian estimator <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> with a uniform prior as Bayes@<math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is related to a naive weighted average accuracy via a positive affine transformation, it turns out the ranking based on the average, denoted as avg@<math id="S2.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math>, is identical to Bayes@<math id="S2.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math> (for the detailed proof, see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Equivalence of Bayesian and Average Rankings for Uniform Prior ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). In the large-trial limit <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the value of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> approaches the average, as expected, but the ranking equivalence holds at all finite <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. This relationship also extends to uncertainty quantification, where the standard deviation of the average relates to the Bayesian standard deviation <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> by a scaling factor, providing a concrete method to compute uncertainty in the average without relying on the Central Limit Theorem (CLT). This is particularly advantageous in small-sample regimes common in LLM evaluations, where CLT-based methods often underestimate uncertainty and produce invalid intervals (e.g., extending beyond [0,1] or collapsing to zero) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bowyer et al. (2025)</a></cite>. As highlighted by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bowyer et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Bayesian approaches with uniform priors (e.g., Beta(1,1) in the binary case) yield well-calibrated credible intervals even for datasets with fewer than a few hundred datapoints, outperforming CLT approximations in coverage and handling complex structures like clustered data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.7 </span>Gold Standard for Ranking</h3>

<div id="S2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Strictly speaking, the underlying true ranking of LLMs for a particular performance metric <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown, because it would require determining the infinite trial limit, <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}=\lim_{N\to\infty}\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}=\lim_{N\to\infty}\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, for each LLM. In practice, we have to settle for an approximation to <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, calculated at some large but finite value <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=N_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=N_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> (for example <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{max}}=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{max}}=80</annotation></semantics></math> in our LLM experiments). Specifically we will use Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N_{\mathrm{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N_{\mathrm{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>—which is the same as the ranking based on avg@<math id="S2.SS7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>—as our “gold standard” or reference ranking. In other words, rankings using smaller <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> will be compared to this gold standard to assess their accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For this comparison, we employ Kendall’s <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, a nonparametric correlation coefficient that measures ordinal agreement between two rankings by comparing the number of concordant and discordant pairs of models. The coefficient ranges from <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> (perfect inversion) to <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> (perfect agreement), with <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> indicating no association. We specifically use the <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> variant, which properly accounts for ties in the rankings (e.g., the intentional tie in our simulation below), ensuring that equivalences do not artificially inflate the correlation. See Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Kendall’s Tau: ‣ F.1 Metrics ‣ Appendix F Experiment setup and Reproducibility ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a> for further discussion and formal definitions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our claims about the gold standard as Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N_{\mathrm{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N_{\mathrm{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, specifically to determine which evaluation methods converge to the true ranking, we conduct a simulation using biased coins as a metaphor for LLMs. In this setup, we already know the underlying performance distribution (the success probabilities <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for each question), allowing us to establish a known ground truth <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Briefly, a biased coin mimic of a stochastic LLM with <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=1</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> values <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the chances of answering each question <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> correctly. These probabilities are drawn from Beta distributions with shape parameters <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Beta}(i,18-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Beta</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Beta}(i,18-i)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> ranging from <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>, with the set for <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=7</annotation></semantics></math> duplicated to create identical performances for two models, and the set for <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=13</annotation></semantics></math> drawn independently for the eleventh model. We generate <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> sets of these <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> probabilities, with <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> values of <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.2332,0.2545,0.3604,0.3642,0.3642,0.4466,0.5418,0.5276,0.608,0.6213,0.7327]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.2332</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2545</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3604</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3642</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3642</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4466</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5418</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5276</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.608</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.6213</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7327</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.2332,0.2545,0.3604,0.3642,0.3642,0.4466,0.5418,0.5276,0.608,0.6213,0.7327]</annotation></semantics></math>, representing different LLMs (note the tie at <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3642" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3642</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3642</annotation></semantics></math> to test handling of equivalent performances). We run experiments for <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=30</annotation></semantics></math> questions, where each LLM “answers” all the questions in each trial according to its success probabilities <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Panel (a) of <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.7 Gold Standard for Ranking ‣ 2 Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows results without bootstrapping: we generate 1000 independent <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, each with <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math> trials; for each step in the number of trials (from <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math>), we compute scores using Pass@<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math> with an unbiased estimator <a href="#A5.E21" title="In Appendix E Extended Related Work ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>), Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math>, a naive Passˆ<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> variant (<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-(1-\hat{p})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-(1-\hat{p})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#A5.E22" title="In Appendix E Extended Related Work ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>), G-Pass@<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\tilde{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\tilde{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A5.E23" title="In Appendix E Extended Related Work ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> with <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\tau}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\tau}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>), and mG-Pass@<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>(<a href="#A5.E24" title="In Appendix E Extended Related Work ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), then derive rankings and compare them to the gold standard using Kendall’s <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> as a measure of rank correlation (where <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates perfect alignment with the gold standard), and report the average <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. Note that we do not explicitly show average accuracy avg@<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> because it is equivalent to Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.p3.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math>, as discussed in section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 Equivalence of Bayesian and Average Rankings for Uniform Prior ‣ 2 Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.
In practice, we are computationally limited to a small number of trials per question. To examine what happens with only <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=80</annotation></semantics></math> trials, we apply two methods of bootstrapping with replacement to the <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, allowing us to estimate how results differ from the ideal case with a large number of independent <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> matrices (panel a). For both methods, we generate <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> bootstrap replicates for each of the <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math> trials, derived from a single <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> matrix. Panels (b) and (c) of Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.7 Gold Standard for Ranking ‣ 2 Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrate this using two bootstrapping schemes. In the first scheme (panel b, column-wise bootstrapping), we resample trial indices; in the second (panel c, row-wise bootstrapping), we resample answers independently for each question. In both cases, the resulting bootstrap replicates are used to recompute evaluation scores, rankings, and <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> values, which are then averaged to produce smoothed convergence curves. The two bootstrapping approaches yield nearly identical behavior, and both closely match the baseline in panel (a). This demonstrates that the <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> convergence behavior is robust and not sensitive to the ordering of answers in either the rows or columns of <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Though in our LLM mimic simulations, we do not have to use bootstrapping (since we can easily generate an arbitrarily large number of <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> matrices), in actual LLM experiments, we have limited trial data, and these results show that bootstrapping provides a viable way of estimating statistical properties like convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As seen in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.7 Gold Standard for Ranking ‣ 2 Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!N</annotation></semantics></math> begins with relatively high agreement with the gold standard and converges much faster to <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> than Pass@<math id="S2.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and its variants, which suffer from greater variance and bias at small <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. All methods eventually converge to the same ranking, but their rates of convergence differ substantially. This makes the convergence rate a crucial factor when choosing between different LLM evaluation methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04265v1/three_panel_biased_coins_10k.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/260;" width="548" height="260"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Kendall’s <math id="S2.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> rank correlation for various evaluation methods compared to the true ranking of <math id="S2.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> sets of biased coins (LLM mimics) with known mean success probabilities <math id="S2.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}=0.2332" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2332</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}=0.2332</annotation></semantics></math>, 0.2545, 0.3604, 0.3642, 0.3642, 0.4466, 0.5418, 0.5276, 0.608 , 0.6213, 0.7327. The simulation evaluates methods including Pass@<math id="S2.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2,4,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2,4,8</annotation></semantics></math>), Bayes@<math id="S2.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, naive Passˆ<math id="S2.F1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, G-Pass@<math id="S2.F1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\tilde{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\tilde{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.F1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\tau}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\tau}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>), and mG-Pass@<math id="S2.F1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> across <math id="S2.F1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.F1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math> trials. Panel a) shows <math id="S2.F1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> results without bootstrapping, while panels b) and c) use two different bootstrapping approaches with <math id="S2.F1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> samples.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS7.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.7.1 </span>Ranking with Uncertainty</h4>

<div id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5 Using Uncertainty Estimates to Decide Significance of Performance Differences ‣ 2 Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we described how uncertainty estimates from the Bayesian approach can be used to evaluate the relative performance of two models. Here, we extend these ideas to incorporate uncertainty into the ranking of multiple models. We do this via our biased-coin LLM mimics, which we denote LLM<sub id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">β</span></sub> for <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1,\ldots,11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1,\ldots,11</annotation></semantics></math>, described in the previous section. To incorporate a chosen confidence interval in the ranking, we order their <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> values from highest to lowest, choose the appropriate <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> threshold (for example <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=1.645" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.645</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=1.645</annotation></semantics></math> for 95% confidence in the ranking), and assign two consecutive methods the same ranking if the absolute <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score falls below this threshold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first row of Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.7.1 Ranking with Uncertainty ‣ 2.7 Gold Standard for Ranking ‣ 2 Bayesian Framework for Evaluating LLM Performance ‣ Don’t Pass@𝑘: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the underlying gold standard ranking for all the LLM mimics, since in this case we know the true <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> values. Note the tie between LLM<sub id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub">4</sub> and LLM<sub id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub">5</sub>, because their <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}=0.3642" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3642</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}=0.3642</annotation></semantics></math> is the same. The second row shows the Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!80</annotation></semantics></math> ranking without a confidence interval (CI) and the third row shows Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!80</annotation></semantics></math> incorporating the <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> CI. The Bayes@<math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!80</annotation></semantics></math> ranking without CI aligns with the gold standard, except for two differences: the order of <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> is swapped, and the tie between <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is not captured, which is expected since this ranking relies solely on <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> estimates without accounting for uncertainty <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, the third row, which incorporates the CI, reveals multiple ties across several models. Interestingly, <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> are now indistinguishable at the <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> CI. Despite the fact that <math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=80</annotation></semantics></math> would be an atypically large number of trials for an actual LLM evaluation, it is insufficient to confidently distinguish the small performance difference (<math id="S2.SS7.SSS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\pi}=0.608" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.608</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\pi}=0.608</annotation></semantics></math> vs. 0.6213) between the two models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of biased-coin LLM mimic rankings based on the gold standard, Bayes@<math id="S2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!80</annotation></semantics></math> without confidence interval (CI), and Bayes@<math id="S2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!80</annotation></semantics></math> with CI.</figcaption>
